+#### Output-sensitive seed search
+
+The maintained search now reuses generation stamps instead of initializing
+all rule counters for every seed set. If no productive unary rule exists,
+all singleton closures are trivial. A growing pair must then be the two
+prerequisites of an original rule that produces a new target, so only
+`O(n)` candidate pairs need closure computations. Proper failed closures
+also exclude later candidate pairs contained in them. This uses a sparse
+candidate graph with `O(n)` memory, preserves the first successful seed and
+its exact derivation trace, and reduces the search bound on this checked
+regime from `O(n^3)` to `O(n^2)` word operations. Inputs with productive unary
+rules retain complete lexicographic pair enumeration with stamped closures.
+Representation arithmetic and certificate replay are unchanged.
+
+On the established 141-crossing Gordian fixture, exhaustive seed search
+changes from 10,011 to 274 actual trials and 4,347,002 to 12,251 work units.
+The paired standalone speedup is 85.86 on nine rounds; the optimized median
+is 3.46 ms. Both exhaustive searches establish only that this particular
+diagram has no two-seed derivation, so the knot verdict remains inconclusive.
+The full group-enabled recognizer still spends about 1.3 seconds in its
+fallback, and its measured speedup is only 1.03. Other whole-pipeline changes
+are mixed, including small regressions; dispatch remains opt-in.
+
+```sh
+python -B -m unittest discover -s tests -p 'test_two_meridian*.py' -v
+python -B benchmark_two_meridian_search.py --rounds 9 --pipeline-rounds 5 \
+  --output results/two_meridian_search_20261008.json
+```
+
+The [proof, frozen baseline, corpus and reproduction notes](two_meridian_research/README.md)
+and [raw paired measurements](results/two_meridian_search_20261008.json)
+include A/A controls, mechanism ablations, exact certificate comparisons and
+separate class-query versus knot-recognition completion counts.
+
